1991 – Green Project- James Gosling and a small team at Sun Microsystems explored software for consumer devices.

1991 – Named "Oak"- The new language is named after an oak tree outside Gosling's office.

1992 – Star7 Demo- A handheld prototype (\*7) shows Oak code running on custom hardware.

1995 – Renamed Java- "Oak" is already trademarked. Java is publicly announced in May 1995.

1996 – JDK 1.0- First official release (January 1996) — Java goes mainstream.

### The Problem

Devices used different chips and operating systems. A C/C++ program had to be recompiled — often rewritten — for each one.

### The Idea: Write Once, Run Anywhere

Compile once into portable bytecode. Any device with a Java Virtual Machine (JVM) can run it — no rewrite needed.

Main Concept: Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA).
